{
  "abstract": "The paper *The Quasi-Riemann Hypothesis: A Zero-Free Half-Plane $\\operatorname{Re}(s)>7/8$* (OpenAI, 30 September 2026) states that every finite-order Hecke $L$-function over $\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-3})$, and hence every Dirichlet $L$-function, has no zero in $\\operatorname{Re}s>7/8$. Paper 1's Appendix B uses this to show that a tenth class-number-one discriminant satisfies $D\\le653$. Part I of that paper proves the $11/12$ half-plane; Part II builds a compensated probe and proves $7/8$. We report an independent check of Part II, conditional on: $\\beta^*\\le11/12$, which Part II takes from Part I and which Paper 79's checked $11/12$ paper also gives; about thirty listed Part I statements, including the proof of the cubic reflection (Proposition 5.1); four imports (Chebotarev, the large sieves of Goldmakher–Louvel and Heath-Brown, and Patterson's cusp expansions as tabulated by Dunn–Radziwiłł); and the Part II steps only read, including the analytic estimates behind the error weights of §§17–18. We found no gap. Each link was followed with its parameter maps, exponent ledgers and error weights recomputed; the compensation of §16 and the initialization of §17 were derived before reading. Identities and ledgers were tested on finite cases, each with a control that must fail: the compensation identities of §16 at every valuation, the ledgers of §§16–20, the Lemma 20.2 certificate, the row counts of Proposition 19.2, and the character sums of §13. We explain the mechanisms: the rescaled term removes a row-independent term only $Q^{-\\operatorname{Re}w}$ below the signal; rows with $p^2\\mid u$ are paid for by a conductor drop; $3/16=l_x/2+b/12$ comes from a Gram loss $P_a^{1/6}$ that counts sixth-power frequencies; and we trace the conditions of Lemma 17.1 and the $6\\kappa$ of Lemma 18.1. Part II uses the $11/12$ theorem itself, through (12.1)–(12.2). This is a conditional, referee-depth check, not a verification, and it proves no new bound.",
